#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "11b5421d-e98b-5e37-9bdd-bec13f010118" :question/math-academy-id "e-21503" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0$." :question/worked-solution "We know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos(\\pi t)\n$$\n\nThus, for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= L{f(t)} \\\\\n&= L{\\cos (πt)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{g(t)} &= L{∫_{0}^{t}\\cos (πτ)dτ} \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s^{2} + π^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221099 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "58ed1920-cd4f-5993-b3bf-1fa4e43492af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21527" :question/problem "For the function\n$G(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1$\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s)$, given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{F(s)} &= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 1}} \\\\\n&= e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{G(s)} &= L^{-1}{(F(s))/(s)} \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{t}e^{τ}dτ \\\\\n&= e^{τ} \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= e^{t} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221104 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "602b037e-28bf-5afd-a466-652668bf08c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21551" :question/problem "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}$." :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nand $A(0) ={0}$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ &= A(τ) \\mid _{0}^{t} \\\\\n&= A(t) - A(0) \\\\\n&= A(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ} &= L{A(t)} = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = A(t) & ⇒ & du = A^{′}(t)dt \\\\ dv = e^{-st}dt & ⇒ & v =-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A(t)dt &= A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}) \\mid _{0}^{∞} - ∫_{0}^{∞}(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})A^{′}(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg \\mid _0^{\\infty} = {0}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using that\n\n$$\nA'(t) = f(t)\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{s}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}A^{′}(t)dt &= \\frac{1}{s}∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s} \\cdot F(s) \\\\\n&= (F(s))/(s)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98e34cc-5bdb-5e57-acd9-dbd3997a6c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220182} {:answer/id #uuid "12084a89-8e50-55e6-9cc7-2e5027bec3d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220183} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad37fd8-a163-51d2-87c2-89d720b65d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220184} {:answer/id #uuid "7529c7b2-5aa7-54ab-80a0-c428e0f14e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(τ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220185}] :answer-field/id #uuid "66a14b47-579f-5747-b81d-d32ee18e01bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220181}] :question/id #uuid "ba1d32c7-91d0-5ea9-ae96-737c7cf8e6a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-331052" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find $L{∫_{0}^{t}f(τ)dτ}$ given $L{f(t)} = F(s)$ for $s > s_{0}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A^{′}(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) = 0$. Then,\n\n$f(τ)$ {{field-1}}"}]}
